      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML Certificate carefully.\n\nThis is a certificate for a measurement transducer (Coriolis mass flow meter sensor + electronics) from Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\n\nKey facts:\n- Certificate number: R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 3\n- Project number: 3800938\n- Pages: 9\n- Member State: The Netherlands\n- Date issued: 11 September 2024\n- Issuing authority: NMi Certin B.V., OIML Issuing Authority NL1\n- Scheme: A\n- Recommendation: R 117-1:2007 \"Dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water\"\n- Accuracy class: 0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5\n- Applicant and Manufacturer: Emerson Process Management Flow B.V., Neostraat 1, 6718 WX Ede, The Netherlands\n- Certified type: measurement transducer, Type: CMFxxx; CMFHCx (xxx or x denoting size of measurement sensor)\n\nComponents:\n1. Measurement sensor (CMF series, CMFHC series)\n2. MVD series electronics (700 Core Processor, 800 Enhanced Core Processor, 800 Remote Dual Core Processor, 1700/2700/2500, 3500/3700)\n3. 5700 electronics\n\nCharacteristics:\n\nTable 1 - General characteristics applicable to the CMF series:\n- Density range: 400 … 1100 kg/m³ → value {min: 400, max: 1100}, unit kg/m³\n- Maximum viscosity: 1080 mm²/s (cSt)\n- Accuracy class: 0.3; 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\n- Environmental classes: M3 / E2+E3 / H3\n- Ambient temperature range: -40 … +55 °C\n- Intended for measurement of: Oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, cryogenic liquids\n\nTemperature range liquid for mass measurement:\n- Classes 0.3, 0.5, 1.0: -10 °C … +250 °C\n- Class 1.5: -200 °C … +50 °C\n\nTemperature range liquid for density and volume measurement:\n- Classes 0.3, 0.5, 1.0 (colspan 3): -10 °C … +150 °C\n- Class 1.5: (blank — so presumably not applicable)\n\nTable 2 - CMF series sensors: CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y, CMF200y, CMF300y, CMF350y, CMF400y\n\nTable 3 - CMFHC series: CMFHC2y, CMFHC3y, CMFHC4y\n\nTable 4 - MVD series electronics general characteristics:\n- Environmental classes: M3/E3/H3 (700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700); M2/E2/H3 (2500)\n- Ambient temp: -40…+55 °C\n- Power supply voltage: 24 VDC; 18…30 VDC; 18…100 VDC / 85…265 VAC, 50…60 Hz\n\nTable 5 - Software versions of MVD series (huge table)\n\nTable 6 - 5700 electronics general characteristics:\n- Environmental classes: M3/E3/H3\n- Ambient temp: -25…+55 °C (if display is primary); -40…+55 °C (if approved external display)\n- Power supply: 21…90 VDC; 100…240 VAC, 50…60 Hz\n\nTable 7 - Software versions of 5700\n\nNow, the model matrix. This is a large family:\n- CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y, CMF200y, CMF300y, CMF350y, CMF400y (CMF series)\n- CMFHC2y, CMFHC3y, CMFHC4y (CMFHC series)\n\nThe \"y\" is a letter indicating the material type.\n\nModel-level attributes: maximum_flow_rate (kg/min), minimum_flow_rate by accuracy class and by core processor (MVD700 vs MVD800), minimum_measured_quantity (kg), maximum_pressure (bar(g)).\n\nThis is complex. The minimum flow rate depends on:\n1. Sensor model\n2. Accuracy class\n3. Core processor (MVD700 or MVD800/5700)\n\nLet me structure this as model_level entries with attributes like:\n- minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3_mvd700\n- minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5_mvd700\n- etc.\n\nOr I could use config_level with axis \"core_processor\" and \"accuracy_class\".\n\nActually let me think about the best representation. The schema allows:\n- model_level[]: attribute × model matrix\n- config_level[]: attribute × config-axis matrix\n\nGiven the minimum flow rate varies by model × accuracy class × core processor, I think the cleanest is to encode model_level entries where the attribute includes accuracy class and core processor, e.g.:\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.3_mvd700_core_processor\" with values per model\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_class_0.5_1.0_1.5_mvd700_core_processor\" (CMF series groups 0.5/1.0/1.5 together)\n\nFor CMF series (Table 2):\nMVD700:\n- Class 0.3: CMF010: 0.033 (H,L,M) / 0.067 (P); CMF025: 0.46; CMF050: 2.72; CMF100: 11.4\n- Class 0.5;1.0;1.5: CMF010: 0.017 (H,L,M)/0.033 (P); CMF025: 0.23; CMF050: 1.36; CMF100: 5.7\n\nFor CMF200/300/350/400 (page 4):\nMVD700:\n- Class 0.3: CMF200: 36; CMF300: 114; CMF350: 453 (A,B,C,E) / 226 (H,L,M,P); CMF400: 680(i)/680(ii)/1700(iii)\n- Class 0.5 (row): CMF200: 18; CMF300: 57; CMF350: 226 (A,B,C,E)/113 (H,L,M,P); CMF400: 680(i)/340(ii)/850(iii)\n- Class 1.0;1.5: CMF400: 680(i)/340(ii)/340(iii); (CMF200, 300, 350 same as class 0.5 due to rowspan)\n\nWait, the rowspan structure: \"Minimum flow rate [kg/min] Accuracy class 0.5;\" has rowspan 2 covering \"18\", \"57\", \"226...\", and the second covered row is \"Minimum flow rate Accuracy class 1.0; 1.5\" which for CMF400 has 680(i)/340(ii)/340(iii). So for CMF200, 300, 350, classes 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 all share 18, 57, 226/113 respectively.\n\nMVD800:\n- Class 0.3: CMF010: 0.033 (H,L,M)/0.067 (P); CMF025: 0.45; CMF050: 1.3; CMF100: 7.83\n- Class 0.5: CMF010: 0.017/0.033; CMF025: 0.23; CMF050: 0.65; CMF100: 3.92\n- Class 1.0;1.5: CMF010: 0.017/0.033; CMF025: 0.18; CMF050: 0.57; CMF100: 2.3\n\nCMF200/300/350/400 MVD800:\n- Class 0.3: 21.7; 73.3; 138.3; 1000(iii)\n- Class 0.5: 10.8; 36.7; 75; 500(iii)\n- Class 1.0;1.5: 7.3; 23; 75; 250(iii)\n\nMinimum Measured Quantity [kg]: CMF010: 0.05; CMF025: 0.5; CMF050: 5; CMF100: 10; CMF200: 20; CMF300: 200; CMF350: 500; CMF400: 500\n\nMaximum pressure [bar(g)]: 25 for all.\n\nTable 3 CMFHC:\nMaximum flow rate: CMFHC2: 12600; CMFHC3: 22000; CMFHC4: 30000\nMVD700:\n- Class 0.3: 1136; 2268; 3400\n- Class 0.5: 568; 1134; 1700\n- Class 1.0: 284; 567; 850\n- Class 1.5: 227; 453; 680\nMVD800:\n- Class 0.3: 491; 1059; 1661\n- Class 0.5: 245; 530; 830\n- Class 1.0: 123; 265; 415\n- Class 1.5: 123; 213; 332\n\nMinimum Measured Quantity: 1000 for all\nMaximum pressure: 25 for all\n\nFootnotes for Table 2:\n- y indicates material type\n- (i),(ii),(iii): CMF400 serial number distinctions\n- (◇): Qmin with MVD700 Core Processor\n- (◇◇): Qmin with MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor and/or 5700 internal core processor\n\nSuperscript letters H, L, M, P and A, B, C, E relate to material letters (y). These are footnote-ish markers. I'll record them where practical.\n\nThe CMF010 values with superscripts: 0.033^(H,L,M) / 0.067^(P) — these are two alternative values depending on the material letter. Similarly CMF350: 453^(A,B,C,E) / 226^(H,L,M,P).\n\nHow to represent? For CMF010 class 0.3 MVD700 minimum flow rate, I could record value as an object or as a string listing alternatives. The schema says value can be number, {min,max}, or string. Alternatives could be a string \"0.033 (H,L,M) / 0.067 (P)\". But the rules say numbers not strings when possible... For these multi-alternative values, I think a string is acceptable, or I could use config_level with axis \"sensor_material\". Hmm.\n\nGiven the complexity, I'll keep it pragmatic: for values with material-dependent alternatives, record the value as a string like \"0.033 (materials H, L, M) / 0.067 (material P)\". Actually, that breaks \"numbers not strings\" but the alternative here isn't a range. Alternatively, use config_level with axis \"material_letter\" for those specific models. That would be a lot of entries. Let me think about what's most useful and schema-conforming.\n\nOption: For CMF010 class 0.3 MVD700, record two config entries:\nconfig_level:\n- attribute: minimum_flow_rate, axis: material_letter (CMF010, MVD700, class 0.3), values: [{condition: \"H/L/M\", value: 0.033}, {condition: \"P\", value: 0.067}]\n\nThat's getting verbose. Maybe simpler: in model_level values, use string for the alternative values with the letters, e.g. {\"model\": \"CMF010y\", \"value\": \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\"}. The rules allow strings for formulas/symbols; alternatives are analogous. I'll do that but note it's an alternative list.\n\nFor CMF400 serial number dependence: values like \"680 (i) / 680 (ii) / 1700 (iii)\". I'll do the same string representation, and record the serial number note in footnotes.\n\nLet me now decide model ids. Models: CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y, CMF200y, CMF300y, CMF350y, CMF400y, CMFHC2y, CMFHC3y, CMFHC4y. I'll use these as model_id (with y as placeholder). model_family.family_name: null or maybe \"CMF / CMFHC\". The type designations are CMFxxx and CMFHCx. I'll set family_name to null since no explicit family name... Actually \"CMF series of measurement sensors\" and \"CMFHC series\" are mentioned. I could set family_name null and include labels. Let me set models with label like \"CMF010y (CMF series measurement sensor)\". Keep it simple: label \"CMF series sensor, size 010\" etc. Maybe too much. I'll give model_id and label attributes minimal.\n\nNow components:\n1. role: \"measurement sensor\", type_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"] or list all models? The certificate page 1 identifies type as CMFxxx; CMFHCx. Components: measurement sensor (CMF series, CMFHC series models), MVD series electronics (variants 700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 2500, 3500, 3700, 800 Remote Dual), 5700 electronics.\n\nComponents with alternatives: The electronics alternatives — MVD700 OR MVD800 OR 5700 (and MVD variants). I'll list:\n- {role: \"measurement sensor\", type_designations: [\"CMF010y\",\"CMF025y\",\"CMF050y\",\"CMF100y\",\"CMF200y\",\"CMF300y\",\"CMF350y\",\"CMF400y\",\"CMFHC2y\",\"CMFHC3y\",\"CMFHC4y\"]}\n- {role: \"electronics (core processor / transmitter)\", type_designations: [\"MVD series: 700 Core Processor\", \"MVD series: 800 Enhanced Core Processor\", \"MVD series: 800 Remote Dual Core Processor\", \"MVD series: 1700\", \"MVD series: 2700\", \"MVD series: 2500\", \"MVD series: 3500\", \"MVD series: 3700\", \"5700 electronics\"], alternatives: \"OR\"}\n\nCharacteristics type_level:\n- density_range: {min: 400, max: 1100}, unit kg/m³\n- maximum_viscosity: 1080, mm²/s (cSt) — unit_symbol \"mm²/s\"\n- accuracy_class: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5\" — it's a list of classes; could be a string. I'll use value as string \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\". Hmm, or record accuracy_classes in recommendation (yes, recommendation.accuracy_classes: [0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]). For characteristics I might include accuracy_class as type-level string. Maybe omit from characteristics since it's in recommendation. But Table 1 lists it. I'll include as string value.\n- environmental_class: \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\" (string)\n- ambient_temperature_range: {min: -40, max: 55}, °C\n- intended_media: string list\n- temperature_range_liquid_mass_measurement: class-dependent! For classes 0.3/0.5/1.0: -10…+250 °C; class 1.5: -200…+50 °C. This is config-level by accuracy class. Use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class\".\n- temperature_range_liquid_density_volume_measurement: classes 0.3/0.5/1.0: -10…+150 °C; class 1.5: blank (not specified/not applicable).\n\nconfig_level entries:\n- {attribute: \"temperature_range_liquid_mass_measurement\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value: {min:-10, max:250}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}, {condition: \"1.5\", value: {min:-200, max:50}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}]}\n- {attribute: \"temperature_range_liquid_density_volume_measurement\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value: {min:-10, max:150}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}]}\n\nmodel_level entries (for sensor models):\n- maximum_flow_rate: values for 11 models, unit kg/min\n- minimum_measured_quantity: 11 models, kg\n- maximum_pressure: 25 bar(g) all — could be type_level? It's in model tables but constant. I'll include as model_level to preserve table fidelity, or type_level with 25 bar(g). I'll put model_level for fidelity... Actually since it's the same for all, type_level is cleaner: maximum_pressure 25 \"bar(g)\". Hmm, but for the CMFHC table it's also 25. I'll make it type_level.\n\nMinimum flow rates — many entries. Let me enumerate attributes:\n\nCMF series, MVD700:\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0.3\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0.5_1.0_1.5\n\nCMF series, MVD800/5700:\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_0.3\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_0.5\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd800_class_1.0_1.5\n\nCMFHC series, MVD700:\n- minimum_flow_rate_mvd700_class_0.3 (CMFHC)\n- ..._class_0.5\n- ..._class_1.0\n- ..._class_1.5\n\nCMFHC series, MVD800:\n- ..._class_0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5\n\nAttribute naming: maybe \"minimum_flow_rate\" with a \"condition\" embedded... The schema for model_level is {attribute, unit_symbol, values[]}. The attribute must encode the class/processor combination. I'll use names like:\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd700\"\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_1.0_1.5_mvd700\"\n- \"minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd800_or_5700\"\netc.\n\nValues with footnote markers: MVD700 minimum flow rates should carry footnote marker \"◇)\" and MVD800 \"◇◇)\". I'll include footnote_markers: [\"◇\"] and [\"◇◇\"]. The markers in doc are (◇) and (◇◇). I'll use \"◇\" and \"◇◇\".\n\nFootnotes array:\n- {marker: \"[1]\", text: \"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"} — from page 1.\n- {marker: \"y\", text: \"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of...\"}\n- {marker: \"(i)\", text: \"Serial number up to 411000\"}\n- {marker: \"(ii)\", text: \"Serial number from 411000 up to 14200000\"}\n- {marker: \"(iii)\", text: \"Serial number higher than 14200000\"}\n- {marker: \"◇\", text: \"Qmin in combination with the MVD700 Core Processor.\"}\n- {marker: \"◇◇\", text: \"Qmin in combination with the MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor and/or 5700 internal core processor.\"}\n\nmatrix_tables: Should I include the flow tables as matrix_tables? The instructions say matrix_tables preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere. The minimum flow rate data is captured in model_level, but the table has 3 dimensions (model × class × processor), which model_level handles via attribute naming. I could also add matrix_tables for Tables 2, 3, 5, 7 for fidelity. That would be huge (software version tables). Hmm. The software version tables are big but they're \"components\"-level characteristics. I could include them as matrix_tables: \"Software versions of the MVD series electronics (700 Core Processor)\" etc.\n\nGiven effort constraints, I'll include matrix_tables for:\n- Table 2 (CMF series characteristics) — but this is largely captured in model_level. Including it as matrix table too is redundant but preserves fidelity. I think it's fine to include the two pages of Table 2 as one matrix table plus Table 3. Actually, to keep response manageable, I'll rely on model_level for Tables 2/3 data and include matrix_tables only for software versions? That's a LOT of rows (about 90 version/checksum pairs).\n\nHmm. The schema says matrix_tables for \"multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Software versions fit nowhere else. But the volume is large. Let me consider: 700 Core Processor: ~21 versions; 800 Enhanced: ~29; 800 Remote Dual: 7; 1700/2700/2500: ~7 + 6 + 6ish; 3500/3700: ~17; 5700 transmitter: ~16; internal core: ~17; PIC: 1; LCD PIC: 2.\n\nI could encode software versions as component characteristics as string lists. E.g., components entry for MVD 700 Core Processor with characteristics {\"software_versions\": \"2.0 (51FF), 2.1 (2B3F), ...\"}. That's compact but ugly.\n\nAlternatively include matrix_tables with rows for each version. That's a big JSON but doable. I think including the software tables as matrix_tables is the right fidelity call, but let me gauge length. Each row: {\"component\":\"700 Core Processor\",\"version\":\"2.0\",\"checksum\":\"51FF\"}. ~100 rows × ~70 chars = 7KB. Acceptable.\n\nActually, let me reconsider — is it necessary? The task says \"preserve multi-dim tables that don't fit elsewhere\". Software version tables are component-level data. I could put them under components[].characteristics as matrix-ish. The schema for components: {role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}}. characteristics is an object; I can put \"software_versions_and_checksums\" as a nested structure. That's flexible.\n\nI'll do this: components for each electronics family:\n- {role: \"core processor (MVD series)\", type_designations: [\"700 Core Processor\"], characteristics: {\"software_versions\": [...]}} — hmm nested lists.\n\nSimplest robust approach: matrix_tables with name \"Software versions — 700 Core Processor\" etc., columns [\"version\",\"checksum\"], rows list. Multiple matrix_tables entries. Let me count entries:\n\n1. Software versions — MVD 700 Core Processor (21 rows)\n2. Software versions — MVD 800 Enhanced Core Processor (29 rows)\n3. Software versions — MVD 800 Remote Dual Core Processor (7 rows)\n4. Software versions — MVD 1700/2700/2500 (mixed; with footnote rows for no-checksum versions)\n5. Software versions — MVD 3500/3700 (17 rows)\n6. Software versions — 5700 Transmitter Software (W&M) (16 rows)\n7. Software versions — 5700 Internal Core Processor (17 rows)\n8. Software versions — 5700 PIC Firmware (1 row)\n9. Software versions — 5700 LCD PIC Firmware (2 rows)\n\nThat's a lot. Total ~120 rows. Hmm, this is getting very long but it is data fidelity. Alternatively I can compress: one matrix_table \"Software versions of the MVD series and 5700 electronics\" with columns [component, version, checksum]. That's one table, ~120 rows.\n\nLet me estimate output size. The model_level section: 11 models × several attributes. maximum_flow_rate (11 values), min measured qty (11), min flow rates: CMF 5 attribute-groups × 8 models = 40 values; CMFHC 8 groups × 3 = 24 values. Fine.\n\nSoftware: ~120 rows. I'll include them. Actually, let me be a bit selective: the checksums for version rows without checksum (1700/2700/2500 with * and **) — I'll include as rows with checksum null and footnote marker.\n\nLet me now also handle Table 2/3 as matrix_tables? The model_level covers it. I'll skip duplicating Tables 2 and 3 as matrix_tables since model_level fully captures them. But multi-dimension (class × processor) is embedded in attribute names — acceptable.\n\nHmm, but the guidance example (R137) shows extracting both matrix_tables AND model_level. For R117, the tables are big. I think model_level is the primary and sufficient. I'll add matrix_tables for software only. Actually, let me also reconsider: maybe put minimum flow rate tables as matrix_tables too, to preserve the exact layout including material-dependent superscripts. The model_level with string values preserves that. I'll go with model_level only for flows.\n\nNow let's define model_level entries concretely.\n\nModels (11): CMF010y, CMF025y, CMF050y, CMF100y, CMF200y, CMF300y, CMF350y, CMF400y, CMFHC2y, CMFHC3y, CMFHC4y.\n\n1. maximum_flow_rate, unit kg/min:\n- CMF010y: 1.8\n- CMF025y: 36\n- CMF050y: 110\n- CMF100y: 450\n- CMF200y: 1450\n- CMF300y: 4500\n- CMF350y: 4920\n- CMF400y: 6804\n- CMFHC2y: 12600\n- CMFHC3y: 22000\n- CMFHC4y: 30000\n\n2. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd700, unit kg/min, footnote ◇:\n- CMF010y: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\" — string with material alternatives. Hmm, can't be number. OK.\n- CMF025y: 0.46\n- CMF050y: 2.72\n- CMF100y: 11.4\n- CMF200y: 36\n- CMF300y: 114\n- CMF350y: \"453 (A, B, C, E) / 226 (H, L, M, P)\"\n- CMF400y: \"680 (i) / 680 (ii) / 1700 (iii)\"\n\n3. minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_1.0_1.5_mvd700, kg/min, ◇:\n- CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n- CMF025y: 0.23\n- CMF050y: 1.36\n- CMF100y: 5.7\n- CMF200y: 18\n- CMF300y: 57\n- CMF350y: \"226 (A, B, C, E) / 113 (H, L, M, P)\"\n- CMF400y: \"680 (i) / 340 (ii) / 850 (iii)\" for class 0.5; for class 1.0/1.5 it's \"680 (i) / 340 (ii) / 340 (iii)\". Hmm! The rowspan: class 0.5 row and class 1.0;1.5 row share values for CMF200/300/350 (18, 57, 226/113), but CMF400 differs between class 0.5 and classes 1.0/1.5. So I need to separate class 0.5 from class 1.0/1.5 for CMF400.\n\nLooking at the raw table again:\n```\n<tr><td>Minimum flow rate [kg/min] Accuracy class 0.5;</td><td rowspan=\"2\">18</td><td rowspan=\"2\">57</td><td rowspan=\"2\">226.../113...</td><td>680(i)<br>340(ii)<br>850(iii)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Minimum flow rate [kg/min] Accuracy class 1.0; 1.5</td><td>680(i)<br>340(ii)<br>340(iii)</td></tr>\n```\nSo class 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 for CMF200 (18), CMF300 (57), CMF350 (226/113) are shared; CMF400 class 0.5: 680/340/850; CMF400 class 1.0/1.5: 680/340/340.\n\nSo for the combined attribute \"class 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\" I should note CMF400 separately. Options: split into two attributes:\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_mvd700: CMF200:18, CMF300:57, CMF350:..., CMF400: 680/340/850\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.0_1.5_mvd700: CMF200:18, CMF300:57, CMF350:..., CMF400: 680/340/340\n\nFor CMF010/025/050/100, classes 0.5, 1.0, 1.5 share values (from Table 2 page 3: \"Accuracy class 0.5; 1.0; 1.5\" row). So those models appear in both attributes with the same values. That's fine — duplication is acceptable and accurate.\n\n4. MVD800 (and/or 5700) CMF series:\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd800_5700, ◇◇:\n  - CMF010y: \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\"\n  - CMF025y: 0.45\n  - CMF050y: 1.3\n  - CMF100y: 7.83\n  - CMF200y: 21.7\n  - CMF300y: 73.3\n  - CMF350y: 138.3\n  - CMF400y: \"1000 (iii)\"\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_mvd800_5700, ◇◇:\n  - CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n  - CMF025y: 0.23\n  - CMF050y: 0.65\n  - CMF100y: 3.92\n  - CMF200y: 10.8\n  - CMF300y: 36.7\n  - CMF350y: 75\n  - CMF400y: \"500 (iii)\"\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.0_1.5_mvd800_5700, ◇◇:\n  - CMF010y: \"0.017 (H, L, M) / 0.033 (P)\"\n  - CMF025y: 0.18\n  - CMF050y: 0.57\n  - CMF100y: 2.3\n  - CMF200y: 7.3\n  - CMF300y: 23\n  - CMF350y: 75\n  - CMF400y: \"250 (iii)\"\n\nWait — check page 4 MVD800 rows: class 0.3: 21.7, 73.3, 138.3, 1000(iii); class 0.5: 10.8, 36.7, 75, 500(iii); class 1.0;1.5: 7.3, 23, 75, 250(iii). Yes.\n\nNote: footnote ◇◇ says \"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor and/or 5700 internal core processor\". Good, so attribute name mvd800_or_5700 is justified.\n\n5. CMFHC MVD700 (Table 3), footnote ◇:\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd700 (CMFHC): 1136, 2268, 3400\n- class 0.5: 568, 1134, 1700\n- class 1.0: 284, 567, 850\n- class 1.5: 227, 453, 680\n\nSince CMF and CMFHC are different series, I can either keep separate attributes or merge into the same attribute names (CMF models get values only in the shared ones; CMFHC in theirs). The class 0.3/0.5/1.0/1.5 splits differ (CMF groups 1.0 and 1.5 together; CMFHC has them separate). I'll create separate attributes for CMFHC with suffix _cmfhc? Or merge class 0.3 into one attribute list with 11 models. Merging: minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd700 with all 11 models — values are consistent per model. That works and is elegant. For class 0.5: CMF values same as their 1.0/1.5 except CMF400; CMFHC has distinct 0.5 values. So attribute minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_mvd700: CMF010 \"0.017...\", CMF025 0.23, CMF050 1.36, CMF100 5.7, CMF200 18, CMF300 57, CMF350 \"...\", CMF400 \"680 (i)/340 (ii)/850 (iii)\", CMFHC2 568, CMFHC3 1134, CMFHC4 1700. All in one attribute. \n\nFor class 1.0: CMF010 \"0.017...\", CMF025 0.23, CMF050 1.36, CMF100 5.7 (same as class 0.5), CMF200 18, CMF300 57, CMF350 \"...\", CMF400 \"680 (i)/340 (ii)/340 (iii)\", CMFHC2 284, CMFHC3 567, CMFHC4 850.\n\nHmm wait — for CMF series classes 1.0 and 1.5 share. So attribute class_1.0 and class_1.5 will both have the CMF values repeated. That's accurate per the certificate.\n\nFor class 1.5: CMF010 \"0.017...\", CMF025 0.23, CMF050 1.36, CMF100 5.7, CMF200 18, CMF300 57, CMF350 \"...\", CMF400 \"680 (i)/340 (ii)/340 (iii)\", CMFHC2 227, CMFHC3 453, CMFHC4 680.\n\nOK so merged attribute names:\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd700 (11 values)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_mvd700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.0_mvd700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.5_mvd700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.3_mvd800_5700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_0.5_mvd800_5700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.0_mvd800_5700 (11)\n- minimum_flow_rate_accuracy_class_1.5_mvd800_5700 (11)\n\nMVD800 CMFHC: class 0.3: 491, 1059, 1661; 0.5: 245, 530, 830; 1.0: 123, 265, 415; 1.5: 123, 213, 332. Merge with CMF MVD800 values similarly.\n\nThat's 8 attributes × 11 models = 88 values. Plus max flow (11), min measured quantity (11), max pressure maybe model-level (11) — let me just include maximum_pressure as model_level since the tables list per model (all 25). Actually type_level single value is fine but tables show it per model; I'll do model_level to be safe. Hmm — simpler: type_level maximum_pressure 25 bar(g) applying to all. But CMFHC table also 25. I'll put type_level to reduce size. Wait, actually let me keep model_level for maximum_pressure too — no, that's 11 duplicate entries. type_level: {maximum_pressure: {value: 25, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"}}. Hmm, \"bar(g)\" as unit symbol — fine, unit_id null.\n\n6. minimum_measured_quantity, kg:\nCMF010 0.05, CMF025 0.5, CMF050 5, CMF100 10, CMF200 20, CMF300 200, CMF350 500, CMF400 500, CMFHC2 1000, CMFHC3 1000, CMFHC4 1000.\n\nComponent-level characteristics (electronics):\n\nMVD series (Table 4):\n- environmental classes: M3/E3/H3 for 700, 800, 820, 1700, 2700, 3500, 3700; M2/E2/H3 for 2500 → config-level by electronics variant.\n- ambient temp: -40…+55 °C condensing humidity\n- power supply: 24 VDC; 18…30 VDC; 18…100 VDC / 85…265 VAC, 50…60 Hz → multiple options. Represent as characteristics on the component: power_supply_voltage_options as list of strings? Or config entries. I'll put in component characteristics as objects:\n  - {\"power_supply_voltage_options\": [...]} — characteristics{} is free-form object, so I can do this. But schema strictness: characteristics at type_level uses structured values. For components, I'll follow similar shape.\n\nLet me define component entries:\n\ncomponent 1: role \"measurement sensor\", type_designations: CMF010y...CMFHC4y (11). alternatives null.\ncomponent 2: role \"electronics — MVD series core processor\", type_designations: [\"MVD700 Core Processor\", \"MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor\", \"MVD800 Remote Dual Core Processor\", \"MVD 1700\", \"MVD 2700\", \"MVD 2500\", \"MVD 3500\", \"MVD 3700\"], alternatives \"OR\", characteristics:\n  - environmental_class: config-level by variant? The characteristics{} object per component — I can nest: {\"environmental_class\": {\"M3/E3/H3\": [...models...], \"M2/E2/H3\": [\"MVD 2500\"]}}. Hmm, awkward. Alternatively use strings.\n  \nActually maybe simpler: put electronics characteristics in type_level/config_level of the main characteristics block, using axis \"electronics\". Like:\n\nconfig_level:\n- {attribute: \"environmental_class\", axis: \"electronics_variant\", values: [{condition: \"MVD 700 / 800 / 820 / 1700 / 2700 / 3500 / 3700\", value: \"M3 / E3 / H3\"}, {condition: \"MVD 2500\", value: \"M2 / E2 / H3\"}]}\n- ambient temp for MVD: type_level? It differs between MVD (-40..55) and 5700 (-25..55 display; -40..55 external display). So config_level with axis \"electronics\":\n  - {attribute: \"ambient_temperature_range\", axis: \"electronics\", values: [{condition: \"MVD series\", value: {min:-40,max:55}, unit °C, note condensing}, {condition: \"5700 (internal display as primary indication)\", value: {min:-25,max:55}}, {condition: \"5700 (approved external display as primary indication)\", value: {min:-40,max:55}}]}\n- power supply:\n  - MVD: 24 VDC; 18–30 VDC; 18–100 VDC / 85–265 VAC 50–60 Hz\n  - 5700: 21–90 VDC; 100–240 VAC 50–60 Hz\n\nRepresent as config_level:\n- {attribute: \"power_supply_voltage\", axis: \"electronics_and_option\", values: [\n  {condition: \"MVD series, option 1\", value: 24, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"},\n  {condition: \"MVD series, option 2\", value: {min:18,max:30}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"},\n  {condition: \"MVD series, option 3 (DC)\", value: {min:18,max:100}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"},\n  {condition: \"MVD series, option 3 (AC)\", value: {min:85,max:265}, unit_symbol: \"VAC\"},\n  {condition: \"5700, option 1 (DC)\", value: {min:21,max:90}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"},\n  {condition: \"5700, option 2 (AC)\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit_symbol: \"VAC\"}\n]}\n- {attribute: \"power_supply_frequency\", axis: \"electronics\", values: [{condition: \"MVD series (AC option)\", value: {min:50,max:60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}, {condition: \"5700 (AC option)\", value: {min:50,max:60}, unit_symbol: \"Hz\"}]}\n\nThat works with config_level shape {condition, value} — I'll add unit_symbol per rules.\n\nAlso 5700 environmental class M3/E3/H3 → type_level? It's component-specific. I'll add config entry: environmental_class axis electronics: MVD variants and 5700.\n\nActually I already have environmental_class config by MVD variant; add 5700: condition \"5700\" value \"M3 / E3 / H3\".\n\nSoftware versions: I'll do matrix_tables. Let me build the rows.\n\n700 Core Processor (Table 5 top):\nRows (version, checksum):\n2.0 51FF; 2.7 F666; 3.2 18D0\n2.1 2B3F; 2.8 1DEA; 3.3 B0D1\n2.2 9005; 3.0 D00D; 3.40 73A9\n2.3 D75B; 3.0-ETO17153 97D6; 3.42 F00C\n2.4 474F; 3.11-ETO19413 14AD; 3.50 11AA\n2.5 14AD; 3.12 1F1B; 3.52 3C4A\n2.6 D732; 3.13-ETO18951 8BF8; (blank)\n\nList: 2.0, 2.1, 2.2, 2.3, 2.4, 2.5, 2.6, 2.7, 2.8, 3.0, 3.0-ETO17153, 3.11-ETO19413, 3.12, 3.13-ETO18951, 3.2, 3.3, 3.40, 3.42, 3.50, 3.52 → 20 versions.\n\n800 Enhanced Core Processor:\n3.11 891378AB; 3.91-ETO21156 65F98DD7; 4.60 DDB76E3C\n3.21 9893B999; 3.94 47EB3E10; 4.70 AEB92E3F\n3.30 A73D25DA; 3.96 756C1BFD; 4.80 F1583A44\n3.42 7FA82CE8; 4.00 C582F843; 4.9 6083BF9B\n3.50 D9343F05; 4.02 8D61C368; 5.08 4D368E71\n3.52 132CCB63; 4.14 40860C63; 5.10 82C541D9\n3.6 A9CA4E81; 4.20 2983A9BE; 5.20 BD69FDD6\n3.61-ETO17170 9AA358FF; 4.21-ETO21931 D6349259; 5.22 F4A8D922\n3.7 BE73CD62; 4.40 B280233F; 5.23-ETO45214 B1D70450\n3.71-ETO18982 580D32B6; 4.42 D7BA0841; 5.30 65828884\n3.8 8CA8E7D1; 4.50 6B48C624; 5.33 BF3164F6\n3.81-ETO20775 7931CE3D; 4.51-ETO32353 BC1660E8; 5.40 0218C30B\n3.9 58CB3E0C; 4.51-ETO33244 D7B81135; (blank)\n\nCount: 13 rows ×3 - 1 = 38 versions.\n\n800 Remote Dual Core Processor:\n1.00 52FB 1CF0; 1.30 AC56C460; 1.50 F42A4B2C\n1.10 787951AA; 1.40 8B64EF94\n1.20 3B7249F6; 1.41 073C45F2\n→ 8 versions. Note \"52FB 1CF0\" has a space — keep as string \"52FB 1CF0\".\n\n1700/2700/2500:\nRow 1: \"3.2, 3.3, 3.4, 3.4.1, 3.5.3*)\" (no checksum), \"3.6, 3.7, 4.1, 4.2*)\" (no checksum), \"4.0, 4.1, 4.2**)\" (no checksum)\n5.0/1.0 7A7F0B39; 6.4/1.3 B77B25C9; 7.1/1.3 88FB1B5C\n5.1/1.0 95F0BC47; 6.5/1.3 88FB1B5C; 7.2/1.3 9ECE81F1\n5.12/1.0 A14FBFB9; 6.6/1.3 9ECE81F1; 7.3/1.3 4A5365D4\n5.2/1.0 746CBE79; 6.7/1.3 4A5365D4; 8.0/1.3 1E1467F9\n6.0/1.1 BB615B55; 6.8/1.3 1E1467F9; 8.02/1.3 201465F9\n6.1/1.2 13176BE6; 6.82/1.3 201465F9\n6.11-ETO19266 9B13F21A; 7.0/1.3 B77B25C9\n→ footnote *) versions for 1700/2700 without checksum; **) versions for 2500 without checksum.\n\nWhich column is which? The header row is \"1700 / 2700 / 2500\" spanning; the first row shows three groups: versions for 1700/2700 without checksum (3.2...3.5.3), versions for 1700/2700 without checksum (3.6, 3.7, 4.1, 4.2), and versions for 2500 without checksum (4.0, 4.1, 4.2). Then subsequent columns alternate. It's ambiguous which numbered column belongs to which processor. I'll preserve as matrix table with columns [version, checksum, version, checksum, version, checksum] → rows with version_1/checksum_1, version_2/checksum_2, version_3/checksum_3. To simplify, I'll use columns [\"version\",\"checksum\"] and rows listing pairs, but the three-column layout loses association. Given footnote explains no-checksum versions, I'll represent:\n\nrows for 1700/2700/2500:\n- {version: \"3.2; 3.3; 3.4; 3.4.1; 3.5.3\", checksum: null, footnote_markers: [\"*)\"]}\n- {version: \"3.6; 3.7; 4.1; 4.2\", checksum: null, footnote_markers: [\"*)\"]}\n- {version: \"4.0; 4.1; 4.2\", checksum: null, footnote_markers: [\"**)\"]}\n- {version: \"5.0/1.0\", checksum: \"7A7F0B39\"}\n- ... etc for all 18 pairs.\n\nTotal: 3 + 18 = 21 rows.\n\n3500/3700:\n7.0/1.1 A1C34F1C; 8.1/1.3 4279A001; 8.41-ETO26097 31D36D05\n7.1/1.1 D5783FCF; 8.14/1.3 62F125F2; 8.43-ETO31478 E35DF3C0\n7.2/1.1 20609FD3; 8.2/1.4 368139C5; 8.50/1.5 1C146AF7\n8.0/1.2 158A12BD; 8.21-ETO23686 D507F464; 8.51-ETO22243 B18A0CB3\n8.02-ETO18947 1CC007C4; 8.3/1.4 8F65A9E9\n8.03-ETO19299 2D6104C2; 8.4/1.4 227B10D2\n→ 17 versions.\n\n5700 Transmitter Software (W&M):\n1.20 (1.0) 2DF0D8E9; 3.0 (3.0) 06108400; 4.1 (4.0) AFE0673B\n1.30 (1.1) ADE631BB; 3.1 (3.0) 2DE64BB2; 4.2 (4.0) 627B3E99\n1.85 (2.0) ETO28130 0EA71B41; 3.2 (3.0) 8CB1FE4B; 4.30 (4.0) AC509A54\n2.00 (2.0) 2F52132D; 4.0 (3.0) 0E4997D5\n2.10 (2.0) 23DD3385; 4.07 (4.0) 44477758\n→ 14 versions. Footnote *): transmitter software and W&M software form matched set; W&M has no checksum; W&M licensed.\n\n5700 Internal Core Processor:\n4.02 8D61C368; 4.60 DDB76E3C; 5.20 BD69FDD6\n4.14 40860C63; 4.70 AEB92E3F; 5.22 F4A8D922\n4.20 2983A9BE; 4.80 F1583A44; 5.30 65828884\n4.40 B280233F; 4.90 6083BF9B; 5.33 BF3164F6\n4.42 D7BA0841; 5.08 4D368E71; 5.40 0218C30B\n4.50 6B48C624; 5.10 82C541D9\n→ 17 versions.\n\n5700 PIC Firmware: 8.0 0000DE9C → 1.\n\n5700 LCD PIC Firmware: 3.0 000081D5 (belongs to transmitter 1.20); 3.0 00007442 (1.30 and later) → 2 rows, footnote **).\n\nOK. This is a lot of rows (~130). It's a judgment call on completeness vs. size. The system prompt says \"Don't fabricate\" and preserve tables. I'll include them — it's mechanical data. Actually, let me reconsider: this may make the JSON enormous (~15-20KB). That's acceptable for a data extraction task.\n\nHmm, but is the software version table essential? The schema has matrix_tables specifically for this. I'll include but maybe consolidate into fewer matrix_tables entries:\n1. \"Software versions — MVD 700 Core Processor\"\n2. \"Software versions — MVD 800 Enhanced Core Processor\"\n3. \"Software versions — MVD 800 Remote Dual Core Processor\"\n4. \"Software versions — MVD 1700 / 2700 / 2500\"\n5. \"Software versions — MVD 3500 / 3700\"\n6. \"Software versions — 5700 transmitter (Weights & Measures)\"\n7. \"Software versions — 5700 internal core processor\"\n8. \"Software versions — 5700 PIC and LCD PIC firmware\"\n\nColumns: [\"version\", \"checksum\"]. For LCD PIC add column \"transmitter_software\". For transmitter software, version strings include the W&M part e.g. \"1.20 (1.0)\".\n\nFootnotes:\n- *) for 1700/2700: \"Software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum.\"\n- **) for 2500: \"Software versions for the 2500 which do not have a checksum.\"\n- 5700 table *): \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. Please note that the W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\"\n- 5700 table **): \"The number between brackets, is the transmitter software which belongs to the stated checksum.\"\n\nSince markers collide (same marker text for different tables), footnotes array is global. I'll merge: markers \"*\", \"**\" appear twice with different meanings. I'll name them distinctly in footnotes: \"*) (Table 5, 1700/2700)\" etc. Or keep the marker as-is and concatenate both texts? Better: footnote markers array entries: {marker: \"*)\", text: \"...\"} for 1700/2700 and for 5700 use different text. Collision is awkward. I'll suffix: markers \"*)–Table 5\", \"**)–Table 5\", \"*)–Table 7\", \"**)–Table 7\". Hmm, or just include four footnote entries with markers \"*)\", \"**) (1700/2700)\", ... Let me use:\n- {marker: \"*)\", text: \"Software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum.\"}\n- {marker: \"*) (Table 7)\", text: \"The transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set. The W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\"}\n\nIn rows, footnote_markers: [\"*)\"] etc. It's approximate. Fine.\n\nAlso global footnotes:\n- [1] page 1: \"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"\n- y note (Tables 2 & 3): \"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of. Where there are different possibilities for a characteristic, the actual letter is indicated in superscript.\"\n- (i), (ii), (iii) CMF400 serial number ranges.\n- ◇, ◇◇.\n\nFor values with (i)(ii)(iii), add footnote_markers: [\"i)\", \"ii)\", \"iii)\"]? The markers in text are (i), (ii), (iii). I'll add [\"i\",\"ii\",\"iii\"] as markers... I'll use \"(i)\", \"(ii)\", \"(iii)\".\n\nTest reports: The big table on page 2. Each row: id, date, pages, role. Group headers: \"Measurement sensor\", \"MVD series electronics\", \"5700 electronics\". These are presumably type evaluation / test reports. The schema: test_reports[]: {id, date, pages, role}. Role options: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. Hard to know which. The text says \"The conformity was established by the results of tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". Also documentation folders TC7056-14, TC7057-17, TC8519-4 mentioned — those are documentation files.\n\nI'll classify: older CVN/C-SP/CPC/NMi reports — hard to tell. Maybe treat all as \"test_report\" except NMi-3800938-01 which is likely the type evaluation report for this project (project number 3800938). Actually NMi-3800938-01 dated 11 September 2024 (same as cert issue) with 11 pages — that's likely the latest examination report. Hmm, it could be the Type Evaluation Report. But \"the sample(s) identified in the OIML Type Evaluation Report\" — the TER number isn't explicitly given. I'll mark NMi-3800938-01 as \"type_evaluation_report\" (project matches) and others as \"test_report\". Add \"documentation_file\" entries for TC7056-14, TC7057-17, TC8519-4.\n\nAdd \"group\"/role info? Schema doesn't have a field for which component. I could encode in role... role is constrained to those 4 values. I'll leave it and not add extra fields. Actually adding extra keys is probably tolerated but let me stay within schema. Hmm, I could note component grouping in the report id? No. I'll skip grouping.\n\nDates: format as ISO? \"10 July 2002\" → \"2002-07-10\". I'll convert to ISO dates.\n\nLet me list all reports:\n\nMeasurement sensor:\n1. CVN/201269, 2002-07-10, 80\n2. CVN-410178-01, 2005-12-21, 6\n3. CVN-410178-02, 2005-12-21, 8\n4. CVN-410178-03, 2005-12-21, 8\n5. CVN-410178-04, 2005-12-21, 6\n6. C-SP/603876, 2006-07-12, 23\n7. CPC-802620-1, 2009-06-11, 25\n8. CPC-9200041-1, 2009-10-15, 5\n9. CPC-9200087-1, 2009-10-15, 6\n10. NMi-10200543-1, 2011-01-11, 4\n11. NMi-10200543-2, 2011-04-05, 6\n12. NMi-2591028-01, 2021-09-30, 9\n13. NMi-3073006-01, 2022-11-14, 10\n14. NMi-3073006-02, 2022-11-23, 10\n15. NMi-3800938-01, 2024-09-11, 11\n\nMVD series electronics:\n16. CVN-201269, 2002-07-10, 80 (note: hyphen vs slash vs sensor CVN/201269 — keep as written)\n17. CPC-307228-1, 2005-02-21, 35\n18. CPC-607580-1, 2007-04-26, 32\n19. CPC-610406-2, 2008-01-29, 31\n20. CPC-710466-1, 2008-11-19, 32\n21. NMi-11200214-01, 2011-05-17, 13\n22. NMi-11200345-02, 2011-10-20, 8\n23. NMi-1901208-01, 2018-07-05, 30\n\n5700 electronics:\n24. NMi-14200115-01, 2015-12-04, 68\n25. NMi-14200115-02, 2015-12-04, 52\n26. NMi-14200115-06, 2016-04-22, 21\n27. NMi-15200770-01, 2016-02-04, 9\n28. NMi-2571596-01, 2021-09-30, 38\n\nDocumentation folders:\n- TC7056-14 (measurement sensor)\n- TC7057-17 (MVD series electronics)\n- TC8519-4 (5700 electronics)\n\nRevision history:\n- Initial, 2021-09-30, \"-\"\n- 1, 2022-11-14, changes list\n- 2, 2022-11-23, \"Addition of the CMF010, CMF025 and CMF050 sensors.\"\n- 3, 2024-09-11, \"Lowering Qmin in combination with the MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor or 5700 internal core processor.\"\n\nCertificate: number \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\", revision \"3\" — number field: I'll use \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02 revision 3\"? Schema: certificate.number, and revision_history separate. I'll set number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and maybe include revision... The certificate-level has no revision field per schema, but revision_history captures it. Hmm — but current revision is 3. I'll put number as \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and add nothing else; maybe include \"revision\": \"3\"? Schema doesn't list it at cert level... The rule \"If a field is not present in the markdown, omit it\" refers to markdown fields. Adding a \"revision\" key at certificate level seems reasonable and harmless. I'll add revision: \"3\".\n\nscheme: \"A\". project_number: \"3800938\". page_total: 9. member_state: \"The Netherlands\". date_issued: \"2024-09-11\". oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\" (from \"OIML Issuing Authority NL1\").\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"NMi Certin B.V.\"\n- address_lines: [\"Thijsseweg 11\", \"2629 JA Delft\", \"The Netherlands\"]\n- person_responsible: \"M.Ph.D. Schmidt\"\n- person_title: null (not given; \"Person responsible\" is the label). Omit.\n- phone: \"+31 88 636 2332\"\n- email: \"certin@nmi.nl\"\n- website: \"www.nmi.nl\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: \"NL1\"\n\napplicants[]: [{name: \"Emerson Process Management Flow B.V.\", address_lines: [\"Neostraat 1\", \"6718 WX Ede\", \"The Netherlands\"]}]\nmanufacturers[]: same.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Measurement transducer\" — options given include \"Measuring system\"... The doc says \"measurement transducer\". I'll use \"Measurement transducer\".\n- type_designations: [\"CMFxxx\", \"CMFHCx\"]\n- module_designation: null or \"Not applicable\"? Not mentioned → omit or null.\n- description: \"Coriolis measurement transducer consisting of CMF / CMFHC series measurement sensor with MVD series or 5700 electronics\" — is that fabricating? Coriolis isn't stated. Better: \"Measurement transducer: CMF series and CMFHC series measurement sensors used with MVD series electronics or 5700 electronics, for dynamic measurement of liquids other than water (R117-1).\" Keep close to source. I'll write: \"Measurement transducer consisting of a CMF or CMFHC series measurement sensor combined with MVD series electronics or 5700 electronics. Intended for the measurement of oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, and cryogenic liquids.\" That's all from the doc.\n\nrecommendation: {id: \"R117-1\", edition: 2007, amendment: null, scheme: \"A\", accuracy_classes: [0.3, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]}. Canonical id: \"R117\" or \"R117-1\"? The example says canonical like \"R60\". This is R 117-1:2007. I'll use id \"R117-1\", edition 2007.\n\nNow characteristics.type_level:\n- density_range: {value: {min:400, max:1100}, unit_symbol: \"kg/m³\"}\n- maximum_viscosity: {value: 1080, unit_symbol: \"mm²/s\"} — note \"(cSt)\" in text; unit \"mm²/s (cSt)\". I'll use unit_symbol \"mm²/s\" and maybe note. Keep unit_symbol \"mm²/s\".\n- environmental_class: {value: \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\"} (sensor overall)\n- ambient_temperature_range: {value: {min:-40, max:55}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"} (sensor, Table 1)\n- intended_media: {value: \"Oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, and cryogenic liquids\"} — string value.\n- maximum_pressure: {value: 25, unit_symbol: \"bar(g)\"} — from sensor tables (all models). \n\nconfig_level:\n- temperature_range_liquid_mass_measurement (axis accuracy_class): [{condition: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value: {min:-10,max:250}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}, {condition: \"1.5\", value: {min:-200,max:50}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}]\n- temperature_range_liquid_density_volume_measurement (axis accuracy_class): [{condition: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0\", value: {min:-10,max:150}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}] (class 1.5 blank — not specified)\n- environmental_class (axis electronics): [{condition: \"MVD 700 / 800 / 820 / 1700 / 2700 / 3500 / 3700\", value: \"M3 / E3 / H3\"}, {condition: \"MVD 2500\", value: \"M2 / E2 / H3\"}, {condition: \"5700\", value: \"M3 / E3 / H3\"}]\n- ambient_temperature_range (axis electronics): [{condition: \"MVD series\", value: {min:-40,max:55}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}, {condition: \"5700, internal display as primary indication\", value: {min:-25,max:55}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}, {condition: \"5700, approved external display as primary indication\", value: {min:-40,max:55}, unit_symbol:\"°C\"}] — add note condensing humidity for MVD? I'll put a footnote? Keep condition text simple: \"MVD series (condensing humidity)\". \n- power_supply_voltage (axis electronics_power_supply_option): entries as above.\n- power_supply_frequency (axis electronics): AC options 50–60 Hz.\n\nFor power supply, MVD options: \"24 VDC\", \"18…30 VDC\", \"18…100 VDC / 85…265 VAC, 50…60 Hz\". I'll produce config values:\n{condition: \"MVD series — option 1\", value: 24, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"}\n{condition: \"MVD series — option 2\", value: {min:18,max:30}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"}\n{condition: \"MVD series — option 3 (DC)\", value: {min:18,max:100}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"}\n{condition: \"MVD series — option 3 (AC)\", value: {min:85,max:265}, unit_symbol: \"VAC\"}\n{condition: \"5700 — option 1 (DC)\", value: {min:21,max:90}, unit_symbol: \"VDC\"}\n{condition: \"5700 — option 2 (AC)\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit_symbol: \"VAC\"}\n\naxis: \"electronics_and_supply_option\".\n\nmodel_level: as planned. For CMF400 values with (i)(ii)(iii) add footnote_markers [\"(i)\",\"(ii)\",\"(iii)\"]. For CMF010 and CMF350 material-dependent values, represent value as string, e.g. \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\". Include footnote markers? The superscripts are material letters, explained by \"y\" note. I'll include the string and no footnote markers (the note is in footnotes as \"y\").\n\nHmm wait, one more check on Table 2 page 3, MVD700 rows for CMF010/025/050/100:\nClass 0.3: 0.033^(H,L,M)/0.067^(P); 0.46; 2.72; 11.4 ✓\nClass 0.5;1.0;1.5: 0.017^(H,L,M)/0.033^(P); 0.23; 1.36; 5.7 ✓\n\nMVD800 CMF010/025/050/100:\nClass 0.3: 0.033^(H,L,M)/0.067^(P); 0.45; 1.3; 7.83 ✓\nClass 0.5: 0.017^(H,L,M)/0.033^(P); 0.23; 0.65; 3.92 ✓\nClass 1.0;1.5: 0.017^(H,L,M)/0.033^(P); 0.1