cksum\":\"B77B25C9\"}\n{\"version\":\"6.5/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"88FB1B5C\"}\n{\"version\":\"6.6/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"9ECE81F1\"}\n{\"version\":\"6.7/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"4A5365D4\"}\n{\"version\":\"6.8/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"1E1467F9\"}\n{\"version\":\"6.82/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"201465F9\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.0/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"B77B25C9\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.1/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"88FB1B5C\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.2/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"9ECE81F1\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.3/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"4A5365D4\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.0/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"1E1467F9\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.02/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"201465F9\"}\n\nTable: \"Software versions — MVD 3500 / 3700\"\n{\"version\":\"7.0/1.1\",\"checksum\":\"A1C34F1C\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.1/1.1\",\"checksum\":\"D5783FCF\"}\n{\"version\":\"7.2/1.1\",\"checksum\":\"20609FD3\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.0/1.2\",\"checksum\":\"158A12BD\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.02-ETO18947\",\"checksum\":\"1CC007C4\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.03-ETO19299\",\"checksum\":\"2D6104C2\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.1/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"4279A001\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.14/1.3\",\"checksum\":\"62F125F2\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.2/1.4\",\"checksum\":\"368139C5\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.21-ETO23686\",\"checksum\":\"D507F464\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.3/1.4\",\"checksum\":\"8F65A9E9\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.4/1.4\",\"checksum\":\"227B10D2\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.41-ETO26097\",\"checksum\":\"31D36D05\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.43-ETO31478\",\"checksum\":\"E35DF3C0\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.50/1.5\",\"checksum\":\"1C146AF7\"}\n{\"version\":\"8.51-ETO22243\",\"checksum\":\"B18A0CB3\"}\n\nTable: \"Software versions — 5700 transmitter software (Weights & Measures)\"\n{\"version\":\"1.20 (1.0)\",\"checksum\":\"2DF0D8E9\"}\n{\"version\":\"1.30 (1.1)\",\"checksum\":\"ADE631BB\"}\n{\"version\":\"1.85 (2.0) ETO28130\",\"checksum\":\"0EA71B41\"}\n{\"version\":\"2.00 (2.0)\",\"checksum\":\"2F52132D\"}\n{\"version\":\"2.10 (2.0)\",\"checksum\":\"23DD3385\"}\n{\"version\":\"3.0 (3.0)\",\"checksum\":\"06108400\"}\n{\"version\":\"3.1 (3.0)\",\"checksum\":\"2DE64BB2\"}\n{\"version\":\"3.2 (3.0)\",\"checksum\":\"8CB1FE4B\"}\n{\"version\":\"4.0 (3.0)\",\"checksum\":\"0E4997D5\"}\n{\"version\":\"4.07 (4.0)\",\"checksum\":\"44477758\"}\n{\"version\":\"4.1 (4.0)\",\"checksum\":\"AFE0673B\"}\n{\"version\":\"4.2 (4.0)\",\"checksum\":\"627B3E99\"}\n{\"version\":\"4.30 (4.0)\",\"checksum\":\"AC509A54\"}\nfootnote *) on the table.\n\nTable: \"Software versions — 5700 internal core processor\"\n4.02 8D61C368; 4.14 40860C63; 4.20 2983A9BE; 4.40 B280233F; 4.42 D7BA0841; 4.50 6B48C624; 4.60 DDB76E3C; 4.70 AEB92E3F; 4.80 F1583A44; 4.90 6083BF9B; 5.08 4D368E71; 5.10 82C541D9; 5.20 BD69FDD6; 5.22 F4A8D922; 5.30 65828884; 5.33 BF3164F6; 5.40 0218C30B\n\nTable: \"Software versions — 5700 PIC firmware\"\n{\"version\":\"8.0\",\"checksum\":\"0000DE9C\"}\n\nTable: \"Software versions — 5700 LCD PIC firmware\"\n{\"version\":\"3.0\",\"checksum\":\"000081D5\",\"transmitter_software\":\"1.20\",\"footnote_markers\":[\"**)\"]}\n{\"version\":\"3.0\",\"checksum\":\"00007442\",\"transmitter_software\":\"1.30 and later\",\"footnote_markers\":[\"**)\"]}\n\nColumns for this last: [\"version\",\"checksum\",\"transmitter_software\"].\n\nFootnotes list (global):\n- {marker:\"1\", text:\"With xxx or x denoting the size of the measurement sensor.\"} — marker \"[1]\"? Use \"1)\". I'll use marker \"[1]\".\n- {marker:\"y\", text:\"y is a letter which indicates the type of material the meter is built of. Where there are different possibilities for a characteristic, the actual letter is indicated in superscript.\"}\n- {marker:\"(i)\", text:\"CMF400: serial number up to 411000.\"}\n- {marker:\"(ii)\", text:\"CMF400: serial number from 411000 up to 14200000.\"}\n- {marker:\"(iii)\", text:\"CMF400: serial number higher than 14200000.\"}\n- {marker:\"◇\", text:\"Qmin in combination with the MVD700 Core Processor.\"}\n- {marker:\"◇◇\", text:\"Qmin in combination with the MVD800 Enhanced Core Processor and/or 5700 internal core processor.\"}\n- {marker:\"*)\", text:\"Table 5: software versions for the 1700 / 2700 which do not have a checksum. Table 7: the transmitter software and the Weights & Measures (W&M) software form a matched set; the W&M software does not have a checksum and means W&M is licensed.\"}\n- {marker:\"**)\", text:\"Table 5: software versions for the 2500 which do not have a checksum. Table 7: the number between brackets is the transmitter software which belongs to the stated checksum.\"}\n\nThat handles the duplicate markers.\n\nNow the certificate \"type_designations\" for certified_type: [\"CMFxxx\",\"CMFHCx\"]. Should I expand? The footnote says xxx/x denote size. Keep as is; models enumerated in model_family.\n\nmodel_family:\nfamily_name: null — or \"CMF / CMFHC\". Not explicitly named as family... Tables say \"CMF series of measurement sensors\" and \"CMFHC series\". I'll set family_name: null and include models with labels.\n\nmodels:\n[\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF010y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 010\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF025y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 025\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF050y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 050\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF100y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 100\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF200y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 200\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF300y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 300\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF350y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 350\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMF400y\",\"label\":\"CMF series measurement sensor, size 400\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMFHC2y\",\"label\":\"CMFHC series measurement sensor, size 2\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMFHC3y\",\"label\":\"CMFHC series measurement sensor, size 3\"},\n {\"model_id\":\"CMFHC4y\",\"label\":\"CMFHC series measurement sensor, size 4\"}\n]\n\nThe \"size 010\" interpretation from footnote [1] \"xxx denoting the size\" — fine.\n\nNote: \"y\" placeholder noted in attributes? Add attributes: {\"material_letter\":\"y (placeholder)\"}? Skip; footnotes cover it.\n\nNow let me also consider including sensor tables as matrix_tables. I decided no — model_level covers. But material-dependent strings in model_level lose exactness? They're preserved as strings. OK.\n\nLet me also double-check \"Maximum viscosity: 1080 mm²/s (cSt)\" — unit mm²/s, note (cSt). Fine.\n\nDensity range 400…1100 kg/m³: {min:400,max:1100} kg/m³.\n\nAccuracy class in Table 1: \"0.3; 0.5; 1.0; and 1.5\" — captured in recommendation.accuracy_classes. I'll also add type_level accuracy_class: \"0.3 / 0.5 / 1.0 / 1.5\" as string. Both fine; I'll include the string.\n\nEnvironmental classes sensor: \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\".\n\nNow certificate.page_total: 9. date_issued: \"2024-09-11\".\n\nOne nuance: cert number includes \"revision 3\". I'll set number \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\" and add revision \"3\".\n\nNow assemble JSON. Watch JSON validity: no trailing commas, escape properly. °C, ³, ², ◇ are fine in JSON strings.\n\nunit_symbol strings: \"kg/m³\", \"mm²/s\", \"°C\", \"kg/min\", \"kg\", \"bar(g)\", \"VDC\", \"VAC\", \"Hz\".\n\nValue formats: all decimals with dots: 1.8, 0.033, 0.067, 0.017, 0.05, 21.7, 10.8, 7.3, 73.3, 36.7, 138.3.\n\nLet me write the JSON now.\n\nStructure order: certificate, issuing_authority, applicants, manufacturers, certified_type, model_family, components, characteristics (type_level, model_level, config_level), matrix_tables, recommendation, test_reports, revision_history, footnotes.\n\nTest report roles: The schema role vocabulary: type_evaluation_report | test_report | documentation_file | evaluation_report. For the reports table I'll use \"test_report\" for all listed reports except NMi-3800938-01 which I'll mark \"type_evaluation_report\" (matching project number 3800938, same date as cert). Documentation folders TC... as documentation_file. I'll include a \"component_group\"? Not in schema — skip.\n\nHmm, one consideration: maybe NMi-3800938-01 is the examination/test report for this revision. Marking it type_evaluation_report is a judgment. Alternatively mark all as test_report. The cert says \"tests and examinations provided in the associated reports\". I'll go with type_evaluation_report for NMi-3800938-01 since project number matches and it's most plausibly the TER supporting this cert.\n\nDates for revision history ISO: 2021-09-30, 2022-11-14, 2022-11-23, 2024-09-11.\n\nRevision 1 changes (join with \"; \"):\n\"Addition of liquefied gases; Increased maximum pressure; Lowered minimum density; Mistake in documentation folder number for the measurement sensors corrected; Mistake in minimum flow rates for the CMFHC family of measurement sensors corrected\"\nRevision \"Initial\": changes: null or \"-\". I'll put null. Revision field as string: \"Initial\", \"1\", \"2\", \"3\".\n\nNow config_level power supply: also add note that MVD AC option includes 50-60 Hz via power_supply_frequency entries.\n\nAlso, ambient temp MVD: \"-40… +55 °C; condensing humidity\" → condition \"MVD series (condensing humidity)\".\n\n5700: \"-25…+55 °C (if the display is the primary indication)\"; \"-40…+55 °C (if an approved external display is used as primary indication)\".\n\nAlright. One more characteristic: 5700 environmental classes M3/E3/H3 — included in config environmental_class.\n\nAlso Table 4 env classes mention MVD 820. Include in condition string.\n\nNow, \"characteristics.type_level.maximum_pressure\": from Tables 2/3 (sensor), 25 bar(g). Good.\n\nWrite the final JSON. Keep it valid. I'll be careful with quotes.\n\nAlso \"scheme (A|B)\" at certificate level: \"A\".\n\nproject_number: \"3800938\" (string or number? I'll use string as IDs).\n\nLet me also decide certified_type.module_designation: null. Omit or null — I'll set null.\n\ndescription for certified_type: \"Measurement transducer (CMF / CMFHC series measurement sensor) for dynamic measuring systems for liquids other than water; used in combination with MVD series electronics or 5700 electronics. Intended for the measurement of oil and oil products, alcohol, chemicals, potable liquids, liquefied gases under pressure, and cryogenic liquids.\"\n\nOK, writing it out. Also double-check JSON size ~ manageable.\n\nFor model_level values with footnote markers on CMF400 entries: {\"model\":\"CMF400y\",\"value\":\"680 (i) / 680 (ii) / 1700 (iii)\",\"footnote_markers\":[\"(i)\",\"(ii)\",\"(iii)\"]}.\n\nFor MVD700 entries generally add footnote_markers [\"◇\"] at the attribute level? Schema: values[] items have footnote_markers. Attribute-level markers not in schema. I'll add footnote_markers on each value: that's 44 entries ×... a lot of repetition. Alternatively, since attribute name includes mvd700/mvd800_5700, the processor linkage is already explicit; footnotes ◇/◇◇ add slight extra. I'll add footnote_markers [\"◇\"] to all MVD700 min-flow values and [\"◇◇\"] to MVD800/5700 ones. It's repetitive but explicit. That adds ~88 small repeats. Acceptable.\n\nActually to reduce bloat, I could skip per-value markers since attribute names encode it and footnotes define them. But rules say when a value references a footnote, record marker. The table rows reference ◇ via merged cells. I'll include them.\n\nLet me now write everything.\n\nAlso note: value strings for material alternatives — e.g. \"0.033 (H, L, M) / 0.067 (P)\". Fine.\n\nOne more check: Table 2 min flow CMF100 class 0.3 MVD700 = 11.4; class 0.5-1.5 = 5.7. ✓. CMF100 MVD800: 7.83 / 3.92 / 2.3 ✓.\n\nCMF050 MVD800: 1.3 / 0.65 / 0.57 ✓. CMF025 MVD800: 0.45 / 0.23 / 0.18 ✓.\n\nMaximum flow CMF010: 1.8 (from \"1,8\") ✓.\n\nMin measured quantity: 0.05, 0.5, 5, 10, 20, 200, 500, 500, 1000, 1000, 1000 ✓.\n\nNow the environmental class of sensor Table 1 \"M3 / E2+E3 / H3\" ✓.\n\nFinal check on certificate.number: \"R117/2007-A-NL1-21.02\". \n\nWrite JSON.",
